<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yalta Conference</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Yaltinskaya konferentsiya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yalta Conference, held 4–11 February 1945 near Yalta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> meeting of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a>, Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> and General Secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> on postwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The three agreed that Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> would be split into occupation zones, with a fourth for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s eastern border would follow the Curzon Line, with compensation in the west from Germany. Stalin pledged that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> would enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> three months after the defeat of Germany and join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> dividing Europe, the conference became a subject of intense controversy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yalta-conference.jpg" alt="Yalta Conference" width="330" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US government photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yalta_Conference_(Churchill,_Roosevelt,_Stalin)_(B&amp;W).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Yalta was the second of three major wartime conferences of the Big Three, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in November 1943 and before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> in July 1945. In October 1944 Churchill and Stalin had reached an informal agreement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on Western and Soviet spheres of influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. By February 1945 the Western Allies had liberated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and Soviet forces stood 65 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, having driven the Germans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Roosevelt had asked for a neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> venue, but Stalin, citing his doctors, proposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> resort of Yalta, and the meetings were held in the Livadia, Yusupov and Vorontsov palaces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Roosevelt wanted Soviet help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and Soviet participation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. Churchill pressed for free elections in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Stalin sought a Soviet sphere of influence there for reasons of security, and called Poland a question of honour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was to be demilitarised and denazified, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> partly in forced labour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was to receive a zone carved from the American and British ones, and Churchill, backed by Roosevelt, won it a seat on the Allied Control Council. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> had not been invited.</p><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the pro-Soviet provisional government was to be reorganised on a broader democratic basis, and Stalin promised free elections. The Declaration of Liberated Europe pledged governments chosen by free elections, though Molotov inserted language that weakened its enforcement. Stalin agreed to join the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s surrender, in return for South Sakhalin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuril-islands">Kuril Islands</a>, and a secret voting formula giving permanent members of the Security Council a veto secured Soviet entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Churchill defended the agreement in a three-day parliamentary debate beginning on 27 February 1945, in which 25 members drafted a protest amendment. Western leaders soon doubted Stalin's promises. On 27 March the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> arrested 16 Polish opposition leaders who had been invited to talks on a provisional government. Admiral William D. Leahy remarked that Yalta's language was so vague that the Soviets could stretch it from Yalta to Washington without technically breaking it. The Polish elections of 16 January 1947 preceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s formal transformation into a communist state by 1949. In American politics Yalta was often seen in a bad light, especially by Republicans and by Americans with ties to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yalta Conference” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
